   Survey; US Geol Survey; Penn State Univ; Nanjing Univ; Hohai Univ; Chang
   an Univ; E China Inst Technol; Chinese Acad Geol Sci, Inst Hydrogeol \&
   Environm Geol; China Geol Survey, Hydrogeol \& Environm Geol Ctr; China
   Acad Sci, Inst Geochem; MOE Lab Biogeol \& Environm Geol; Yunnan Bureau
   Geol; Mineral Resources Explorat \& Dev; K C Wong Educ Fdn},
Abstract = {Moshui Lake is a representative of urban lakes in China and heavily
   polluted by heavy metals. Cross-flow ultrafiltration (CFF) studies were
   carried out in 2004 to study the phase speciation for some metals in the
   surface water. The mass-balance experiments prove that it is feasible to
   recover metals by CFF, and the reclaimed percentage of most metals
   (89.30\%similar to 110.03\%) can meet the research requirement. Three
   investigations show a considerable but highly varying part of metals is
   associated with the colloidal fraction. The percentage of alkaline-earth
   metals Ca, Mg, Ba and Sr, and alkali metals K and Na associated with the
   colloidal fraction is small (generally less than 7.1\%), while other
   trace metals investigated range from 10\% to 82\%. Besides their
   inherent characterization, the contents of metals in different size
   distributions are affected by pH, lake water temperature, alkalinity,
   total dissolved solids (TDS) and the degree of contamination.},
