Note = {5th International Symposium on Environmental Hydraulics, Arizona State
   Univ, Tempe, AZ, DEC 02-07, 2007},
Abstract = {Quantitative environmental impact assessment (EIA) provides a tool for
   decision makers and the public to understand issues relating to
   sustainable development. The use of advanced hydro-environmental
   modelling and visualization technology can help to provide an intuitive
   and interactive digital platform to better communicate EIA issues and
   thus promote continuous public involvement and stakeholder engagement.
   Such a platform must be based on a robust hydrodynamic model engine that
   provides seamless predictions from the near to far field. This lecture
   summarizes the key issues of impact assessment in urban coastal cities,
   and presents examples of impact assessment of several major sewage
   outfalls, including the Hong Kong Harbour Area Treatment Scheme. The
   predictions of a recently developed Distributed Entrainment Sink
   Approach (DESA) for dynamic coupling of the near and far fields are
   compared with alternative ``actual source{''} approaches and field
   experiments. The conceptual elements of a real time water quality
   forecast and management system are outlined.},
