   2018},
Abstract = {Air pollution is one of the most urgent problems of modem world,
   eventually causing the premature death of millions people every year.
   One of the burdens due to exposure to air pollutants is a lung cancer.
   It is necessary to develop new effective methods of carcinogen
   monitoring. The aim of the study was to evaluate if spider webs are
   suitable for the assessment of air mutagenicity by Ames MPF (TM) test.
   It is the first time spider webs had been incorporated into Ames MPF
   (TM) procedure. Webs of two spider species Araneus diadematus and
   Agelena labyrinthica have been collected at four sites exposed to high
   pollutants emission. Salmonella typhimurium TA98 strain without
   metabolic fraction have been used for the assay. All samples exhibited
   mutagenic activity most likely due to the road traffic. Webs of A.
   labyrinthica have shown higher mutagenicity effects at the tested sites
   in comparison to A. diadematus, plausibly because of the longer
   exposition time. The results are most promising and indicate high
   potential of combining spider webs and MPF (TM) procedure for assessing
   the mutagenic properties of urban air pollution.},
